        "heading": "14.5.1 Transport of oxygen in blood:",
        "headingUrdu": "موضوع 16",
        "text": "14.5.1 Transport of oxygen in blood:\n\nRed Blood Cell\n\nOxygen from lungs\n\nOxygen released\n\nto tissue cells\n\nApproximately 97% of oxygen is carried by the red blood cells as oxyhaemoglobin, while 3% is transported as dissolved oxygen in the plasma. At high partial pressure of oxygen, oxygen binds with haemoglobin. This binding is a reversible reaction that occurs in the alveoli of the lungs in the presence of enzyme carbonic anhydrase. Each molecule of haemoglobin can bind with four molecules of oxygen to form oxyhaemoglobin.\n\nHb+402 Carbonic anhydrase\n\nHb402\n\nHaemoglobin molecules\n\nOxygen bonded\n\nwith haemoglobin molecules\n\nFig: 14.6 Haemoglobin in red blood cells carry oxygen in the blood.\n\nawaz e inqilab.com\n\nRespiration\n\nChapter 14\n\nThe ability of haemoglobin to bind with oxygen is called oxygen carrying capacity of blood. The oxygen carrying capacity of blood is directly proportional to the partial pressure of oxygen (PO,). Maximum oxygen carrying capacity of arterial blood is 20 ml/100 ml of blood (100% saturated) which is achieved at 100 mmHg PO2.\n\nPO2in tissue at rest\n\nPO2 in lungs\n\n(a)\n\n23%\n\n75%\n\n98%\n\nIn resting tissues, haemoglobin releases some oxygen, which is like partially emptying the glass.\n\nFig: 14.7\n\nHaemoglobin saturated with oxygen in the lungs is ilke a\n\nnearly full glass.\n\nThis is because the amount of haemoglobin is 15 gms/100 ml of blood. Since 1gm Hb can combine with 1.34 ml of O,, therefore 100 ml blood combines with 20 ml O, (100% saturated). Normally each 100 ml of arterial blood contains 19.4 ml 0, (i.e. it is 97% saturated; PO, is 95 mmHg), while 100 ml of venous blood contains 14.4 ml O, (i.e. it is 75% saturated; PO, is 40 mmHg). Thus, 5 ml of O, is released to the tissues by each 100 ml blood.\n\nDuring exercise, the need of oxygen is greatly increased in the tissues so more oxygen is released by the arterial blood to the tissues. The venous blood that leaves an active tissue has only 4.4 ml O, per 100 ml of blood (20% saturated; PO, is 18 mmHg). Compared to carbon dioxide, oxygen is relatively insoluble in the blood, therefore a small amount of O, is transported in dissolved state in the plasma. Normally each 100 ml blood contain 0.29 ml O, (PO, is 95 mmHg) and this capacity may increase up to 0.3 ml/100 ml blood at 100 mmHg PO,.\n\nRespiration\n\nPOz in tissue during exercise\n\nPO2 in lungs\n\nChapter 14\n\n73%\n\n25%\n\nIn exercising tissues, haemoglobin releases more oxygen, which is like emptying most of the glass.\n\n98%\n\nHaemoglobin saturated with oxygen in the lungs is like a\n\nnearly full glass.\n\nFig: 14.8\n\nWhile the 100 ml of venous blood has 0.12 ml of dissolved O, (PO, is 40 mmHg). Thus, 0.17 ml of O, is transported by each 100 ml blood through the tissues per cycle in the dissolved state.",
