        "heading": "15.11.1 Thermoregulation in Human",
        "headingUrdu": "موضوع 46",
        "text": "15.11.1 Thermoregulation in Human\n\nThe ability to regulate body temperature is critical to sustain normal life. Death is the ultimate result if the body temperature strays to far from the normal. Core body temperature, the temperature of structures below the skin and subcutaneous tissue, should be maintained between 36.4 and 37.3 °C.\n\nRespiratory metabolism is major source of heat energy in human. This energy is mainly released by breakdown of carbohydrates and fats. The process of heat production in organisms is called Thermogenesis. This process is regulated by nervous system and hormones. There are two types of thermogenesis, shivering thermogenesis and non-shivering thermogenesis.\n\nShivering thermogenesis involves repeated stimulation of voluntary muscles by motor neurons. It produces shivering response in muscles which can increase heat production by up to five times the basal level.\n\nNon-shivering thermogenesis is the heat production caused by the high metabolic rate. Thyroxin, a thyroid hormone increases metabolic rate which result in heat production. Its effects are long term. Adrenaline produces short term increase in metabolic activity.\n\nHomeostasis",
