        "heading": "17.7 EFFECTS OF DRUGS ON NERVOUS COORDINATION",
        "headingUrdu": "موضوع 30",
        "text": "17.7 EFFECTS OF DRUGS ON NERVOUS COORDINATION\n\nThere are many drugs which are being used to treat nervous disorders, but abuse/misuse of these drugs can produce ill effects on the nervous coordination. One should be aware of uses and abuses of some common drugs used in our society. 17.7.1 Heroine:\n\nHeroin is an illegal, highly addictive drug. Heroin is processed from morphine, a naturally occurring substance extracted from the seed pod of certain varieties of poppy plants.\n\nMedical use:\n\nUnder the chemical name diamorphine, diacetylmorphine, the heroine is prescribed as a strong analgesic, where it is given via subcutaneous, intramuscular, or intravenous route. Its use includes treatment for acute pain, such as in severe physical trauma, myocardial infraction, post-surgical pain, and chronic pain, including end- stage cancer and other terminal illnesses. Abuses/Misuses\n\nHeroin is usually injected, sniffed/snorted, or smoked. With heroin, short term effects include warm flushing of the skin, dry mouth, depressed respiration, and suppression of pain, nausea, vomiting, severe itching, and spontaneous abortion. Long term effects of heroin use include addiction, infectious diseases, for example, HIV/AIDS and hepatitis B and C, collapsed veins, bacterial infections, abscesses, infraction of heart lining and valves, and arthritis.",
