        "headingUrdu": "موضوع 11",
        "text": "20.3.2 Proliferative/preovulatory phase (Days 6-14):\n\nUnder the influence of rising blood levels of estrogens, the basal layer of the endometrium generates a new functional layer. Consequently, the endometrium once again becomes velvety, thick, and well vascularized. Normally, cervical mucus is thick and sticky, but rising estrogen levels cause it to thin and become crystalline, forming channels that facilitate the passage of sperm into the uterus.\n\nmature graafian antrum filled with\n\nfollicle\n\ndeveloping\n\nfollicles\n\nprimary\n\ncocyle\n\nliquor follicule\n\novulation\n\nuterine gland\n\nOVARY\n\ncorpus luteum of menstruation\n\nvenous lacunae endometrial vein\n\nspiral artery\n\nbasal artery\n\narcuate artery\n\nUTERUS\n\nmenstrual proliferative\n\nphase\n\nphase\n\nsecretory phase\n\n26 28 premenstrual phase\n\nFig: 20.10 Phases of Menstrual Cycle.\n\nOvulation, which takes less than five minutes, occurs in the ovary at the end\n\nof the proliferative stage (day 14) in response to the sudden release of LH from the anterior pituitary. LH also converts the ruptured follicle to a corpus luteum.",
