        "text": "22.2.2 Incomplete dominance:\n\nWhen neither of the two alleles expresses independently in heterozygous state, rather a blend of expression of both alleles is appeared is called incomplete dominance. In 1899 Carl Correns was working on a flowering plant named 4 O'clock. When he crossed a true breeding red flowered plant with a true breeding white flowered 4 O'clock, all the F, hybrids had pink flowers. This novel phenotype had a shade intermediate between those of the parents due to an intermediate amount of pigment in petals. When Correns self- fertilized F, pink, the F, showed all three phenotypes of flower in the ratio of 1 red: 2 pink: 1 white. Red was homozygous for red alleles.\n\nP Generation\n\nRed RIR1\n\nWhite R2R2\n\nGametes R\n\nGametes 2 R12 R2\n\nand white was homozygous for F, Generation white alleles. But when allele for red and allele for white were present together in the same plant. neither of them masked the effect of other, rather these alleles showed incomplete dominance in the form of pink colour. As there is no truly dominant allele, the usual\n\ncapital and small letter distinction F, Generation for dominant and recessive trait is not necessary. Both the alleles are represented by the same letter \"R\" but are numbered differently to distinguish white from red.\n\nPink\n\nR1R2\n\n1/2 Ri V2R1 Eggs Red\n\n1/2 R2\n\nSperm\n\nRTR\n\n1/2 Rar\n\nPink Pink\n\nR1R2 RiR2\n\nWhite R2R2\n\nFig: 22.4 Incomplete dominance\n\nAllele for red is designated as R, and the allele for white as R... Punnett square indicates that the phenotypic ratio is the same as the genotypic ratio.\n\nInheritance\n\nThere is absolutely no need of test cross.",
