        "heading": "26.5.4 Maxam-Gilbert Method:",
        "headingUrdu": "موضوع 12",
        "text": "26.5.4 Maxam-Gilbert Method:\n\nChapter 26\n\nIn 1976-1977, Allan Maxam and Walter Gilbert developed a DNA sequencing method which is also called as chemical cleavage method because it is based on chemical modification of DNA and subsequent cleavage at specific bases. Procedure:\n\nThe DNA to be sequenced must first be end labeled, at one end only. This is accomplished by kinase treatment with 32P ATP, which transfers radioactive phosphorus from ATP to 5' end of each strand. Dimethylsulphoxide (DMSO) is then added and the labeled DNA samples heated to 90°C. This results in the breakdown of base pairing and dissociation of DNA molecules into its two component strands.\n\n(a) DNA Radioactiv label\n\n(b) sc\n\nRadioactive label\n\n5' (\"P)\n\nG\n\nG+A\n\nT+C\n\nC'\n\n5'-labeled SSDNA\n\nSequencing gel\n\nElectrophoresis\n\nReading\n\nPICCGAGGTCCCTGTT\n\nDeduced\n\nDNA Sequence\n\nFig: 26.17 Maxam and Walter Gilbert\n\ndeveloped a DNA sequencing method.\n\nThe two strands are separated from one another by gel electrophoresis, one strand is purified from the gel and divided into four samples (G, A+G, T+C, and C), each of which is treated with one of the cleavage reagents and modifying chemical reagents.\n\nBiotechnology",
